self: reported in 65s; flock-check: reported in 65s\nplan check: PASS \u2014 no deviation; the what is covered\n\nFindings: the flock fixes are sound and match the reproduction. flock-check, util-linux 2.39.3: under -o a grandchild that outlives the crashed command does not hold the lock. A second `-o -w 2` launch fails after 2.00 s. Fifty lslocks and /proc/locks reads during a launch never make it fail. lslocks prints the path, while /proc/locks needs the lock file's inode. All three other items are resolved (:482, :468-471, :577-580), and no new contradiction appears. Four consider items:\n- consider (self): :457-458 \"`-w 2` rides out a concurrent probe\" is stale now that probes do not take the lock. -w 2 now only covers two racing launchers: say so, or drop it.\n- consider (self): :577-580 a late arrival read during closing may need a QUESTION round, but open ids were already converted to incoming tickets. Say that a late arrival gets a verdict or is converted too.\n- consider (flock-check C): under -o only the flock process holds the lock. A SIGKILL to flock alone frees it while Pi keeps running, and a second guichet could start. Stopping by tmux session or systemd unit (:589-590) is safe. The card `pid` (:466) should be Pi's, and a stop should never target flock alone. Worth one clause in the phase 1(a) audit (:678).\n- consider (flock-check E): if the lock file is unlinked and recreated while held, a second flock on the new inode succeeds, so two guichets can run. Nothing deletes it today. One sentence, \"the lock file is never deleted\", would keep it that way.\nUnresolved simplify: not planned\n\nseat: self \u00b7 runtime=claude-code \u00b7 family=anthropic \u00b7 route=self \u00b7 model=anthropic/claude-opus-5-5 \u00b7 status: ran\n"}

